  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThe cold front and its associated upper level trough responsible\nfor the showers/thunderstorms on Wednesday is now well east of \nthe WFO MPX coverage area, with appreciable cold and dry air \nadvection on going behind it for the Upper Midwest. Temperatures\nwill bottom out in the lower 30s to lower 40s this morning \nfollowed by highs only topping out in the upper 40s to upper \n50s. While this is still above normal for late October, this is \nobviously cooler than the temperatures experienced yesterday \ndespite plentiful sunshine for today. Similar temperatures are \nexpected on Friday with the same high pressure airmass shifting \neast across the area through late day Friday. The western extent\nof the departing cold front, located over the Central Plains, \nwill push northward a bit Friday night through Saturday, \nbringing rain on Saturday for mainly southern MN but potentially\nas far north as an Alexandria-Mora line. No thunderstorms are \nexpected, just scattered to numerous rain showers.\n\nA better chance for rain still looks to come Monday into Tuesday\nas a system develops over the lee of the Rockies into CO/KS. \nHowever, with models still projecting a generally straight \neasterly track, this will keep the center of the system south of\nMN/WI but still thrust a large precipitation shield to its \nnorth across our coverage area for Monday through Tuesday, \nresulting in a persistent soaking rainfall out of high moisture \ncontent and strong isentropic lift. In addition, guidance \ncontinues to tamp down temperatures from getting too far over \nnormal. Highs Sunday-Monday will range in the upper 50s to mid \n60s as this system moves across the region. Behind it, highs \nwill return to the 50s for the middle of next week along with \nless active weather.",
